{"@context":["https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant/ns/content/v1.jsonld",{"@base":"https://litlfred.github.io/folio/","folio-assistant-core":"https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant-core/v0/ns/"}],"@id":"papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/rem-kelvin-classical-limit","@type":["folio-assistant-core:Remark","doco:Section"],"label":"rem:kelvin-classical-limit","kind":"remark","title":"Classical limit of the $q$-Kelvin dispersion","uses":["papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/prop-kelvin-dispersion-derivation"],"interprets":"papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/prop-kelvin-dispersion-derivation","tags":["classical-limit","kelvin-wave","self-induction"],"text":"kelvin-classical-limit.md","provenance":"authored","html":"<a id=\"rem:kelvin-classical-limit\"></a><p>As <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">q \\to 1</code>, the components resolve to their classical definitions: <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">[k]_q \\to k</code>, <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\Gamma_q \\to j(j+1)</code>, and <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\nu_q \\to 0</code>. The <a href=\"#prop:kelvin-dispersion-derivation\" data-at=\"fluid-dynamics/turbulence-acoustics\">q-Kelvin dispersion</a> immediately recovers the classical form <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\omega_k = \\Gamma k^2 / (4\\pi a^2) \\cdot (\\ln(L/a) - c)</code>. The Biot--Savart self-induction integral generates the classical logarithmic factor; the <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">q</code>-deformation subsumes this logarithm directly into the deformed core radius <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">a_q</code>.</p>\n","source":"folio/quantum-observable-universe/fluid-dynamics/kelvin-classical-limit.md","heading":"Remark","number":"20.3.7","page":"b/rem-kelvin-classical-limit","qa":{"counts":{"pass":60,"fail":0,"warn":0,"na":17,"unknown":0,"stale":0},"milnor":{"total":2,"notPassing":0}}}
